textosLlegan("theory", {"hf-sospecha":{"html":"<p>Until modernity, philosophy believed in a <strong>subject</strong> who is master of himself: an ‘I’ identified with consciousness and possessing the will as the faculty of freedom. <strong>Marx</strong>, <strong>Nietzsche</strong> and <strong>Freud</strong> —the <strong>‘masters of suspicion’</strong>, in Paul Ricoeur’s phrase— put that idea into crisis: the subject is not ‘master in his own house’. Beneath what we say and think there are hidden forces that we do not control. Marx is suspicious from the standpoint of economics and history; Nietzsche, from the genealogy of values; Freud, from psychic life. At the end of the topic we will see what unites the three and what separates them.</p>\n\n<h2>Before suspicion: 19th-century thought</h2>\n<h3>The promise of modernity, turned into crisis</h3>\n<p>The 19th century cannot be studied as a list of authors: placing Hegel, Schopenhauer, Darwin or the suffragists side by side does not yet mean understanding the century. Its core is that the image modernity had of itself enters into crisis. The Enlightenment united reason, freedom and progress; the 19th century asked whether that union is truly universal, or whether history, class, gender, language, biology and power play a part in it.</p>\n<p>That is why this block is a bridge. On one side are Kant and the project of the Enlightenment (Topics 18 and 19): the rational subject, universal law, autonomy and critique. On the other will appear Marx, Nietzsche and Freud (next, in this same topic): thinkers who call into question the transparent explanations that consciousness gives of itself. In between, the 19th century shifts the ground of thought. The human being is not only abstract reason: it is a being situated in language, in a people, in the world of work, in the body, in the tension of desire and in biological evolution.</p>\n<p>The central question of the century is not ‘who is each author?’, but ‘what happens to modernity when its promises collide with the real historical world?’. Freedom has to be realised in institutions, but institutions can also hold power. History can be understood as progress, but in the name of progress colonialism and exclusion can be justified. Science breaks old myths, but in the name of science new ideologies can also be born.</p>\n<p>To understand the century, before the authors we must see these five shifts.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Shift</th><th>What changes?</th><th>Philosophical consequence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>From reason to history</td><td>The subject is placed in an age and in a language</td><td>Universality becomes problematic</td></tr><tr><td>From nature to culture</td><td>Peoples, customs and languages gain weight</td><td>Human nature becomes historicised</td></tr><tr><td>From harmony to conflict</td><td>Institutions are not always neutral</td><td>Domination and alienation appear</td></tr><tr><td>From the human being to the continuum of life</td><td>Darwinism weakens human exceptionality</td><td>Anthropocentrism is called into question</td></tr><tr><td>From consciousness to suspicion</td><td>Ideas can hide interests or forces</td><td>The ground is prepared for Marx, Nietzsche and Freud</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Romanticism, history and the people</h3>\n<p>The Enlightenment stressed universal reason: all human beings, in principle, can use the same reason and be subject to the same moral law. But at the beginning of the 19th century <strong>Romanticism</strong> and <strong>historicisation</strong> opened up another question: does reason really appear the same in all languages, peoples and ages?</p>\n<p>The contribution of <strong>Herder</strong> is fundamental. For him, the human being is not first an abstract subject who then enters a culture: it is formed in its language, in its memory, in its customs and in the life of its people. That is why the ‘people’ (<em>Volk</em>) is not a mere numerical sum. A people has a way of life born in time: songs, myths, institutions, the experience of its territory and its own way of naming the world.</p>\n<p>Vico and Montesquieu, already in the 18th century, can be read in the same direction. Vico showed that peoples create their own symbolic worlds and that history is not the mere application of abstract ideas. Montesquieu, for his part, stressed the influence of climate, geography, the economy and material ways of life on laws and customs. Alongside them, Hamann’s critique is important: there is no pure reason outside language. Thinking is always thinking in words, in the body, in a tradition and in a community.</p>\n<p>This perspective corrects the Enlightenment, but it also has risks. Defending the value of the people and of culture can recognise diversity; but if the people is understood as a closed organic totality, exclusionary nationalism or the idealisation of tradition can arise. Nineteenth-century thought thus inherits a double tension: abstract universality is not enough, but neither can a particular culture be made sacred without criticism.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: if we want to defend universal rights, how do we avoid taking one particular culture as the measure of all humanity?</p>\n<h3>Hegel: history, dialectic and freedom</h3>\n<p><strong>Hegel</strong> is one of the great bridges of the 19th century. After Kant, German idealism took up this question: how can we overcome the break between subject and object, between phenomenon and noumenon, between theory and praxis? Hegel’s answer is that reality must be understood as a <strong>process</strong>. Truth is not a motionless object given from the start, but a historical totality that develops itself.</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"hf-sospecha-17\">Through <strong>dialectic</strong>, a historical form or a concept reveals its own limits. That limit becomes contradiction, and contradiction generates a new form. School explanations use thesis, antithesis and synthesis, although Hegel’s thought cannot be reduced to that mechanical scheme. The key is that contradiction is not a simple error: it is the engine of movement.</p>\n<p>For Hegel, history is the development of the <strong>consciousness of freedom</strong>. Freedom is not just doing what I want: it has to be realised in institutions. Here law, morality, the family, civil society and the state come in. Objective spirit shows that freedom is not a mere individual inner feeling, but a way of life that takes shape in recognition, norms and institutions.</p>\n<p>But Hegel’s strength is also his risk. Reading history as a rational process gives a tool for criticising reality: it makes contradictions visible. At the same time, it can tempt us to justify the existing order as ‘a necessary moment of history’. That is why the Hegelian tradition split. The right-wing Hegelians stressed reconciliation and the closure of the system; the left-wing Hegelians used contradiction for critique. Marx will start from this latter path.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">‘The owl of Minerva takes flight only when dusk is falling.’ — Hegel, <em>Elements of the Philosophy of Right</em>, Preface (1820)</blockquote>\n<h3>Master and slave: the dialectic of recognition</h3>\n<p>The most famous passage of Hegel’s <em>Phenomenology of Spirit</em> (1807) is the dialectic of master and slave (or lord and bondsman). When two self-consciousnesses meet, each seeks the <strong>recognition</strong> of the other. In the struggle, one risks life and becomes the master; the other, out of fear of death, submits and remains the slave. But a paradox arises: the master lives depending on the slave’s work, and the slave, working on nature, transforms himself and transforms the world. Thus Hegel shows that true freedom does not come from domination, but from work and mutual recognition. This passage has had an enormous influence: Marx took it up to think about labour and exploitation; existentialism and feminism, to analyse the relationship between recognition and domination.</p>\n<h3>Objective spirit: family, civil society and state</h3>\n<p>For Hegel, freedom is not only the private will of the individual: it is realised in institutions, at the three levels of <strong>objective spirit</strong>. The first is the <strong>family</strong>, based on love and natural unity. The second is <strong>civil society</strong>: the sphere of the particular interests of individuals, of work and the market, where needs are satisfied but inequalities and conflicts also arise. The third and highest is the <strong>state</strong>: not the mere sum of private interests, but the moment in which rational freedom is realised in a common institution. According to Hegel, the individual fulfils his freedom in the rational state, within general laws. But from this also comes a risk: exalting the state too much can obscure individual freedom. That tension will recur in the great political debates of the 19th and 20th centuries.</p>\n<h3>The ambivalence of history: freedom and domination</h3>\n<p>After Hegel, history can no longer be thought as before. Reality is not a collection of fixed essences, but a process of becoming, conflict and institutions. This gives modernity great critical force: the contradictions of society are not superficial defects, but tensions produced by the social structure itself.</p>\n<p>However, the philosophy of history can become dangerous. If it is said that history has a single direction, modern Europe easily becomes the universal measure of history. Other peoples are no longer read as different historical paths, but as degrees of ‘backwardness’. This often served to justify colonialism, scientific racism and violent modernisation.</p>\n<p>Here is born the hard political question of the 19th century: is progress truly the extension of freedom, or a language for imposing new forms of domination in the name of freedom? Marx will read history from the standpoint of class struggle; Nietzsche will see wills to power beneath the supreme values; Freud will place the conflict inside the subject itself. Without Hegel that suspicion would not have been born in the same way, but Hegel’s own system is also subject to suspicion from within.</p>\n<p>Hegel cannot be merely celebrated or merely rejected: we must understand his ambivalence.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Hegel’s legacy: contribution</th><th>What does it allow?</th><th>Risk</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>History as process</td><td>Thinking reality in its becoming</td><td>Imposing a single goal</td></tr><tr><td>Contradiction</td><td>Understanding critique and movement</td><td>Reconciling conflict too quickly</td></tr><tr><td>Institutions</td><td>Seeing the social conditions of freedom</td><td>Excessively justifying the state</td></tr><tr><td>Universality</td><td>Generalising the idea of freedom</td><td>Naturalising Eurocentrism</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: does understanding history as a rational process help us to criticise oppression better, or does it bring the risk of justifying the existing order?</p>\n<h3>Comte and positivism</h3>\n<p>During the nineteenth century, the boundless faith that scientific modernity places in science finds its most radical expression in the <strong>positivism</strong> of <strong>Auguste Comte</strong> (1798-1857). According to this thinker, only that which rests on <strong>empirical observation</strong> and <strong>verifiable facts</strong> deserves the name of authentic knowledge; any metaphysical speculation is ruled out. In his view, humanity must overcome the theological and metaphysical stages in order to reach the positive stage, in which facts, laws and their scientific description take priority. A form of knowledge that has reached maturity does not go astray by enquiring into ultimate essences, but devotes itself to discovering the laws linking some facts with others.</p>\n<p>This vision, which unites ‘order and progress’, will mark a good part of 19th-century culture and politics. Positivism carries the prestige of science over into the organisation of society: society too is understood as a reality that can be studied, measured and reformed. Thus the confidence in science born at the beginning of modernity takes on a new social and political form.</p>\n<h3>Comte’s positivism: science, order and social reform</h3>\n<p>Comte’s positivism is not just an abstract praise of science. In the context of industrialisation, and the disorder and social conflicts of the 19th century, it is also a programme for reorganising society. According to Comte, philosophy must be based on concrete facts, on experience and on laws; otherwise, the sterile constructions of metaphysics do not help the progress of humanity. That is why positivism is practical from the start: it wants to know the structure of society in order to govern it better.</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"hf-sospecha-37\">At the centre of his vision is the <strong>law of the three stages</strong>. First, <strong>theological</strong> explanations dominate (facts are explained by gods); then <strong>metaphysical</strong> ones (by abstract essences); finally, in the <strong>positive</strong> stage, science describes facts and seeks their laws. That is why Comte unites ‘order’ and ‘progress’: a peaceful and fairer society will come not from blind faith or mere speculation, but from positive knowledge.</p>\n<h3>From positivism to sociology</h3>\n<p>Comte’s project took a new step: he argued that not only nature but also society should be studied scientifically. Thus <strong>sociology</strong> was born, with the intention of analysing collective behaviour, institutions and conflicts from the standpoint of laws. That idea had great influence, because for the first time in modernity it was seriously proposed that the human world too could be organised according to the scientific method.</p>\n<p>Yet right here another of modernity’s tensions appears: to what extent is it possible to subject the whole of human life to measurement and law? In the 19th century that question remains open, and that prepares the ground well for the critique of suspicion in Marx, Nietzsche and Freud.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: think of a current social problem and write how Comte would want to analyse it. Then add a limit that you can see: what does a strictly positivist view leave out?</p>\n<h3>Darwin: the human being, removed from the centre of nature</h3>\n<p>What <strong>Darwin</strong> sets out in <em>On the Origin of Species</em> (1859) goes far beyond a biological explanation of how species arise. From a philosophical point of view, it represents a shock to the image that the human being had formed of itself. If Copernicus displaced the Earth from the central place in the universe, Darwin placed the human being within the continuity of living beings. It can no longer be seen as a metaphysical exception foreign to nature: the human being is the product of a process of evolution.</p>\n<p><strong>Natural selection</strong> explains the change of species without requiring a moral end or a transcendent plan. This is very powerful, because it removes finality from the way of explaining the world. Nature need not have the human being as its goal. Evolution stresses adaptation, variability and the relationship with the environment, not a moral perfection.</p>\n<p>But Darwin’s theory cannot be directly turned into social policy. When the ‘struggle for survival’ is used as a moral norm for society, <strong>social Darwinism</strong> is born: a biological explanation becomes an ideological justification of power and inequality. Darwin’s strength lies in breaking anthropocentrism; its ideological uses, by contrast, must be criticised.</p>\n<p>Darwin prepares the climate of suspicion, but on another level. Marx will analyse the structures of society; Nietzsche, the affective and political origin of values; Freud, the unconscious. Darwin shows that the elevated story human beings told about themselves is not enough: we are historical and biological animals, and that lowers the pride of reason.</p>\n<h3>Suffragism: the limit of modern freedom</h3>\n<p>The crisis of the 19th century is not just a conflict between abstract theories. The language of modern rights spread, but the subject of those rights was often imagined as male, property-owning, white and European. That is why <strong>suffragism</strong> is fundamental: when modernity speaks in the name of ‘man’, who is included in that ‘man’?</p>\n<p>American suffragism developed in connection with the abolitionist movement. At the Seneca Falls convention (1848), organised among others by Elizabeth Cady Stanton, women’s political rights were demanded in an organised way; later, leaders such as Stanton and Susan B. Anthony stood out. In Britain, Emmeline Pankhurst founded the Women’s Social and Political Union in 1903, with more striking and combative strategies.</p>\n<p>The work of <strong>Harriet Taylor Mill</strong> is especially important. In her writings on the emancipation of women she defended education without discrimination, equal job opportunities and public participation. Linked to John Stuart Mill (Topic 17), she put the liberal theory of freedom itself to the test: if freedom is universal, it cannot be limited according to sex.</p>\n<p>Suffragism sheds light on the main tension of the 19th century. There may be a language of progress, but we must ask whom that progress leaves out. Freedom is not just a nice concept: it needs the vote, education, access to work, equality before the law and the right to appear in public space.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">Women need education, public participation and equality before the law. — Central idea of Harriet Taylor Mill, <em>The Enfranchisement of Women</em> (1851)</blockquote>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Investigate: compare American and British suffragism (context, methods, leaders and results) and explain why the vote is the test of modern citizenship.</p>\n<h3>From ideology to suspicion</h3>\n<p>At the end of the 18th century, the word ‘ideology’ had a positive meaning: the science of ideas. In the context of Destutt de Tracy, the aim was to analyse the origin and functioning of ideas in order to overcome prejudices and superstitions. In that Enlightenment confidence, ideas could be clarified and truth could be sought by rational methods.</p>\n<p>The 19th century changes the direction of the word. <strong>Ideology</strong> is no longer just the study of ideas: it is also the false idea, the naturalised appearance or the expression of a hidden interest. For Marx, the dominant ideas of an age may be the ideas of the ruling class. Nietzsche will look behind the supreme values of morality for affects, attitudes to life and wills to power. Freud will take suspicion to the interior of the subject: consciousness is not the only master of the house.</p>\n<p>That is the goal of this block: the 19th century does not reject reason, but places it in its conditions. It is not enough to know what an idea says. We have to ask who says it, from what position, what institution makes it credible, what interest it covers up and what historical force feeds it.</p>\n<p>That is why this block prepares the thought of suspicion that comes next. Marx, Nietzsche and Freud do not fall from the sky. Hegel’s history, Schopenhauer’s blind will, Darwin’s biological decentring, the political critique of suffragism and Romantic historicisation are the paths that, before them, crack the security of modernity.</p>\n<h3>How to read the bridge</h3>\n<p>The best way to study this block is not to put each author in a separate box. It is better to follow the same question again and again: what happens to the modern subject when it sees that it is historical, social, biological and conflictual?</p>\n<p>With Hegel we see that freedom has to be sought in history and in institutions; Marx will start from there, but take it to material conditions. With Schopenhauer, darker desires and sufferings appear behind reason; Nietzsche will start from there, but seeking a tragic affirmation instead of the negation of life. With Darwin, the human being loses its metaphysical privilege; this forces us to rethink culture and morality alongside the history of nature.</p>\n<p>Suffragism poses another test: if the language of modern freedom is true, the political subject cannot be limited to half the population. And the critical sense of ideology gives the final key: ideas do not only describe the world; they can organise it, cover it up or call it into question.</p>\n<p>This table brings together the main threads of the block, so as not to study the authors as a loose list.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Revision map: philosophical thread</th><th>What does the 19th century show?</th><th>What does it prepare?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>History and culture</td><td>That reason is situated in a language, a people and an age</td><td>The critique of abstract universality</td></tr><tr><td>Hegel and the dialectic</td><td>That freedom is realised in institutions and historical conflicts</td><td>Marx’s historical materialism</td></tr><tr><td>Schopenhauer</td><td>That behind the rational subject there is blind desire and suffering</td><td>Nietzsche’s rereading of life and values</td></tr><tr><td>Darwin</td><td>That the human being is situated in the evolutionary history of life</td><td>The crisis of anthropocentrism</td></tr><tr><td>Suffragism</td><td>That modern rights can contain political exclusions</td><td>The feminist critique of citizenship</td></tr><tr><td>Ideology</td><td>That ideas can cover up interests and power relations</td><td>The critical method of suspicion</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Three confusions to avoid</h3>\n<p>Do not confuse historicisation with mere relativism. The 19th century does not say that all truths are worth the same; it shows that truth, reason and rights always appear in historical and social conditions.</p>\n<p>Do not confuse Hegel’s dialectic with an automatic scheme. Thesis, antithesis and synthesis help, but the key is that a form generates its own limit and that this limit sets thought in motion.</p>\n<p>Do not confuse Darwin with social Darwinism. Darwin gives a powerful biological explanation of life; social Darwinism uses that explanation to justify social inequality, and that is an ideological use.</p>\n<table class=\"glos\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Historicisation</td><td>The human being, reason and institutions are not merely forms outside time: they are realities that are born, change and develop in conflict within historical processes.</td></tr><tr><td><em>Volk</em></td><td>In German Romanticism, the community of the people, with a shared language, memory, customs and historical experience.</td></tr><tr><td><em>Kultur</em> and <em>Zivilisation</em></td><td><em>Kultur</em> stresses the historical way of life and spiritual creation of a people; <em>Zivilisation</em>, technical progress, institutions and material organisation.</td></tr><tr><td>Dialectic</td><td>A way of understanding reality as process: each form generates its limit, that limit becomes contradiction, and from contradiction a new form is born.</td></tr><tr><td>Objective spirit</td><td>In Hegel’s system, the level at which freedom is realised in institutions: law, morality, family, civil society and state.</td></tr><tr><td>Master and slave</td><td>Hegel’s famous dialectic: the struggle for recognition between two self-consciousnesses. The slave, through work, opens the way to freedom; a great influence on Marx and on existentialism.</td></tr><tr><td>Recognition</td><td>Being accepted by the other as a free and equal subject. For Hegel it is the social condition of freedom.</td></tr><tr><td>State (Hegel)</td><td>The summit of objective spirit: the sphere in which rational freedom is realised in a common institution, above the family and civil society.</td></tr><tr><td>Sublation (<em>Aufhebung</em>)</td><td>The movement of the dialectic: contradiction is not eliminated, but at once preserved and overcome at a higher level.</td></tr><tr><td>Cunning of reason</td><td>The idea that the passions of individuals, without intending it, fulfil the general end of history (the development of freedom). Figures such as Napoleon are instruments of reason.</td></tr><tr><td>Owl of Minerva</td><td>‘The owl of Minerva takes flight at dusk’: philosophy understands events after they have happened, not beforehand.</td></tr><tr><td>Positivism</td><td>Only what is empirically verifiable is knowledge; it rejects metaphysical speculation (Comte).</td></tr><tr><td>Law of the three stages</td><td>According to Comte, humanity passes from theological explanation to metaphysical and from there to the positive stage, in which facts and laws dominate.</td></tr><tr><td>Social Darwinism</td><td>An ideology that uses Darwin’s biological explanation to justify social inequality, colonialism or ruthless economic competition.</td></tr><tr><td>Limited citizenship</td><td>When a society that uses the language of universal rights does not, in practice, recognise some groups as full political subjects.</td></tr><tr><td>Biological decentring</td><td>Placing the human being within the evolutionary history of life, instead of understanding it as an exception above nature.</td></tr><tr><td>Suspicion</td><td>Distrusting the explanation that consciousness gives of itself. It does not deny truth: it looks for the conditions, interests and forces beneath the surface meaning.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n\n<h2>Marx: economic suspicion</h2>\n<h3>A material critique of modern society</h3>\n<p><strong>Karl Marx</strong> (1818-1883) is not just the author of a political slogan. His thought is a philosophical, historical and economic critique of modern society. The central question is not put in a simple way, such as ‘is capitalism good or bad?’. It is a sharper question: how are work, property, the market and power organised in capitalist society, and why do these historical relations appear as if they were natural? That is why Marx’s philosophy is a form of <strong>social suspicion</strong>. The transparent explanations that consciousness gives of itself are not enough: to understand freedom, law, merit, religion or personal success, we must examine the material conditions in which they arise.</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"hf-sospecha-73\">Ideas do not disappear, but neither do they live in the air: they are situated in social relations. This is Marx’s central contribution. Modernity promised freedom and equality, but industrial capitalism turned labour power into a commodity. Subjects who are equal before the law enter the market in very unequal conditions. If the worker has to sell his capacity to work in order to live, legal equality can become abstract. The Marxist critique begins there: <strong>formal freedom</strong> and <strong>material dependence</strong> can appear together.</p>\n<p>Marx is therefore suspicious of society’s ideas and morality: they are not universal truths, but a <strong>superstructure</strong> that justifies the power of the ruling class.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">‘The wealth of societies in which the capitalist mode of production prevails presents itself as an enormous heap of commodities.’ — Karl Marx, <em>Capital</em>, vol. I (1867), ch. 1</blockquote>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Marx’s questions: field</th><th>Question</th><th>Key concept</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Philosophy</td><td>How can we understand history through its contradictions?</td><td>Materialist dialectic</td></tr><tr><td>Anthropology</td><td>How does work become a force against oneself?</td><td>Alienation</td></tr><tr><td>Economics</td><td>How is value created and accumulated?</td><td>Surplus value</td></tr><tr><td>Society</td><td>Who controls the means of production?</td><td>Class struggle</td></tr><tr><td>Politics</td><td>What should critique do?</td><td>Praxis</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Marx’s analysis does not have a single level: it unites philosophy, economics and politics.</p>\n\n<h3>Hegel and Feuerbach: two decisive precursors</h3>\n<p>Marx is a thinker after <strong>Hegel</strong>, but not a mere follower of his. From Hegel he takes the way of understanding reality as process: history is not an accumulation of loose facts, but a dynamic that moves through contradictions. In Hegel’s <strong>dialectic</strong>, reality is understood through tensions and sublations. Marx keeps that idea, but changes the engine: the movement of history is not the self-consciousness of Spirit, but the way in which human beings produce their material life. That is why it is said that Marx saw Hegel ‘upside down’ and wanted to set him on his feet (he says so in the afterword to the second edition of <em>Capital</em>, 1873). In Hegel’s system it is ideas, reason or Spirit that guide history; for Marx, by contrast, the material life of human beings, work, property and class relations condition consciousness. The dialectic is not rejected: it is carried over from idealism to materialism.</p>\n<p><strong>Feuerbach</strong> opened another path: he understood religion as a <strong>projection</strong> of the human being (<em>The Essence of Christianity</em>, 1841). Human beings project onto God their strengths, their desires and their ideals, and then become subject to that creation of their own. Marx accepts that critique, but says that it is not enough. It is not enough to say that human beings create God; we must ask why they need that consolation. In Marx, the critique of religion has to become a critique of society.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Hegel, Feuerbach and Marx: thinker or source</th><th>What does it contribute?</th><th>Marx’s transformation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hegel</td><td>Thinking history through contradictions</td><td>Carrying the dialectic over to material conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Feuerbach</td><td>Understanding religion as a projection of the human being</td><td>Examining the social and economic roots of the projection</td></tr><tr><td>Political economy</td><td>A vocabulary for analysing labour, value and capital</td><td>Bringing the structure of exploitation to light</td></tr><tr><td>French socialism</td><td>The demand for equality and emancipation</td><td>Moving from moral critique to historical analysis</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Marx does not repeat his precursors: he places his strength on another terrain.</p>\n\n<h3>Alienation: work against oneself</h3>\n<p><strong>Alienation</strong> is one of the most important concepts of the young Marx (<em>Economic and Philosophic Manuscripts</em>, 1844). The human being is the animal that <strong>works</strong>: work, in itself, can be the activity with which it transforms the world and objectifies its capacities. Through work it humanises nature, puts its strength and imagination into products and fulfils itself. But under capitalism the worker does not experience that product as his own: the product appears on the market, subject to private property, foreign to the worker and often turned into his enemy.</p>\n<p>Economic alienation has four dimensions. The worker becomes estranged from the <strong>product</strong>, because the product is not his; from the <strong>work process</strong>, because work is not self-realisation but a forced activity done in exchange for a wage; from his own <strong>creative capacity</strong>, because work, instead of enriching life, becomes a means of survival; and from his relationship with <strong>other people</strong>, because cooperation becomes market competition.</p>\n<p>This is not a mere subjective feeling. The worker is not alienated just because he ‘feels bad’: alienation lies in the objective structure of the mode of production. When the product, the work process and labour power are subject to the logic of capital, the worker’s activity becomes a power that turns against him. That is why the problem cannot be solved with individual therapy: we must examine the organisation of work, property and economic power.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>The four dimensions of economic alienation</th><th>What is lost?</th><th>How does it appear under capitalism?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The product</td><td>The result of work</td><td>The product becomes the property of capital</td></tr><tr><td>The activity</td><td>The meaning of work</td><td>Work is experienced as a forced activity</td></tr><tr><td>Human capacity</td><td>Creativity and self-realisation</td><td>Work is a mere means of survival</td></tr><tr><td>Others</td><td>Cooperation</td><td>Relationships become competition and market exchange</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Alienation is not simply a psychological state: it is tied to the structure of work.</p>\n\n<h3>Religion, ideology and social consolation</h3>\n<p>Marx’s critique of religion is not a simple anticlerical caricature. Religion can give meaning and consolation to suffering. That is why it has social force: it is not a pure lie, but the expression of real suffering and, at the same time, a way of bearing it. This is what his famous formula condenses: religion is ‘the <strong>opium of the people</strong>’ (<em>Introduction to the Critique of Hegel’s Philosophy of Right</em>, 1844). The problem is that this consolation can numb the strength to transform this world: it consoles the oppressed with a reward in the hereafter and prevents them from rebelling.</p>\n<p>For Feuerbach, religion is a projection of the human being. For Marx, by contrast, that projection has to be explained: what society needs such a consolation? What material situations create the need to seek happiness in another world? That is why the critique of religion cannot stay at the level of consciousness: the conditions of social suffering must be changed.</p>\n<p><strong>Ideology</strong> can perform a similar function, but in a wider field. It is not a simple collection of conscious lies. It is the way a social organisation presents itself as natural, just or sensible. If it is said that poverty is only the consequence of a lack of individual effort, or if private property is presented as a law of nature, a historical structure is being naturalised.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: a discourse that gives meaning to social suffering can help, but at what point does it become an instrument for maintaining oppression?</p>\n\n<h3>Historical materialism</h3>\n<p><strong>Historical materialism</strong> understands societies on the basis of their <strong>modes of production</strong>. How a society produces, who controls the resources, who works and who appropriates the surplus: that conditions the form of political, legal and ideological institutions. History is not a mere succession of kings, ideas or great figures; it is also the history of social conflicts and of relations of production. Its engine is the <strong>class struggle</strong>.</p>\n<p>Marx does not say that ideas do not matter. He says that, in order to understand them, we must examine their social place. Human beings make history, but they do not make it under conditions of their own choosing. The modes of production, the forms of property and the inherited class relations open some possibilities and close others. That is why historical materialism is not a simple mechanical determinism. Material conditions condition consciousness, but social struggle and praxis can transform history. Conditions do not erase the subject: they show where the subject is situated. That is why Marx’s analysis is critical: it seeks to make people see the structures that condition their lives.</p>\n<p>The engine of historical change is an internal <strong>contradiction</strong>: the <strong>productive forces</strong> (technology, labour, knowledge) tend to grow, while the <strong>relations of production</strong> (property, forms of domination) tend to become fixed. When the latter become a fetter on the former, an epoch of social revolution opens and a new order is born. This is how classical Marxism reads the succession of modes of production (Marx himself speaks of Asiatic, ancient, feudal and bourgeois modes): <strong>primitive communism</strong>, <strong>slave</strong> society, <strong>feudal</strong> and <strong>capitalist</strong> society, which should be followed by a classless communist society.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Reading a mode of production: question</th><th>What is examined?</th><th>Marx’s concept</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>How is it produced?</td><td>Technology, labour and organisation</td><td>Productive forces</td></tr><tr><td>Whose hands hold the resources?</td><td>Land, machines, the factory, capital</td><td>Property</td></tr><tr><td>Who works?</td><td>Labour power and working time</td><td>Proletariat</td></tr><tr><td>Who receives the surplus?</td><td>Profit and accumulation</td><td>Capital</td></tr><tr><td>What discourse justifies it?</td><td>Law, morality, religion, the story of merit</td><td>Ideology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>For Marx, the first question for understanding a society is not what it says about itself, but how it produces.</p>\n\n<h3>Base and superstructure</h3>\n<p>Marx distinguishes between <strong>base</strong> and <strong>superstructure</strong>. The base is the economic foundation of society: the productive forces, the organisation of work, property and the relations of production. The superstructure, by contrast, is the set of legal, political, religious, philosophical and cultural forms that rise on that foundation.</p>\n<p>This distinction is not a rigid line. The superstructure is not a mere ornament: laws, the state, school, morality or religion organise society and can help to sustain the economic structure. But they are not born in a vacuum: they reflect, conceal or organise the material interests and conflicts of a concrete society. That is why Marx says that it is not the consciousness of human beings that determines their being, but their social being that determines their consciousness. That sentence does not turn the human being into a puppet. It changes the starting point: to understand ideas we must ask in what conditions of life they arose, what class made them credible and what power relations they help to maintain.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Base and superstructure: level</th><th>Components</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Base</td><td>Work, technology, property, relations of production</td><td>Organising the material foundation of society</td></tr><tr><td>Legal superstructure</td><td>Law, rights, contract</td><td>Regulating and legitimising social relations</td></tr><tr><td>Political superstructure</td><td>State, administration, power</td><td>Maintaining or transforming the social order</td></tr><tr><td>Ideological superstructure</td><td>Religion, morality, culture, education</td><td>Making historical structures seem sensible or natural</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The relationship is not automatic, but it is the key to Marx’s social suspicion.</p>\n\n<h3>Social class and class struggle</h3>\n<p><strong>Social class</strong> is not a mere level of income. For Marx, class is defined by position in relation to the means of production. The <strong>bourgeoisie</strong> controls capital, the factory, machines or the main means of production; the <strong>proletariat</strong>, by contrast, has to sell its labour power in order to live. That is why the relationship between the two classes is not easy to reconcile: one is organised to appropriate the surplus; the other is obliged to sell its labour power.</p>\n<p>The <strong>class struggle</strong> is not the name of a moral hatred. It is the historical form of opposed interests within a mode of production. Wages, the working day, the pace of work, safety, property, strikes or laws are not merely technical discussions: they are situated in the power relations between classes. For Marx, that conflict is one of the main engines of modern history.</p>\n<p>However, being a worker does not automatically generate <strong>class consciousness</strong>. Ideology, fear, competition and daily needs can cloud the situation. When class consciousness arises, workers no longer see their problem as a personal failure, but as the consequence of a common structure. That is why Marx’s analysis is political: making the structure of society visible is the condition of collective action.</p>\n\n<h3>Commodity, value and surplus value</h3>\n<p>To understand capital, Marx starts from the <strong>commodity</strong>. A commodity has <strong>use value</strong>, because it satisfies a need, and <strong>exchange value</strong>, because on the market it is related to other commodities. The peculiarity of capitalism is that labour power itself becomes a commodity: the worker sells his capacity to work.</p>\n<p>From this is born <strong>surplus value</strong>. During the working day, the worker creates more value than he receives in his wage. That difference is appropriated by the capitalist, and from it comes the logic of capitalist accumulation. <strong>Exploitation</strong> is not just a personal fraud: it is a structure that can exist even within a ‘free’ contract, when labour power becomes a commodity.</p>\n<p>A simple example helps. If a worker creates 100 units of value in a working day and receives 40 in wages, the remaining 60 fall within the realm of surplus value. It is not a purely moral question: it shows that the accumulation of capital is based on the organisation of the worker’s working time.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Outline of surplus value: moment</th><th>What happens?</th><th>Consequence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sale of labour power</td><td>The worker offers his capacity to work</td><td>Labour power becomes a commodity</td></tr><tr><td>Working day</td><td>The worker creates new value</td><td>The product remains in the hands of capital</td></tr><tr><td>Wage</td><td>The reproduction of labour power is paid for</td><td>The worker can live, but does not receive all that he has created</td></tr><tr><td>Surplus value</td><td>The capitalist appropriates part of the value created</td><td>Capitalist accumulation is possible</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The figures are teaching examples; what matters is to understand the structure.</p>\n\n<h3>Commodity fetishism</h3>\n<p><strong>Commodity fetishism</strong> adds another layer. On the market, things appear as if they had value in themselves, but that value comes from the labour relations between people. What looks like a neutral relation between things is, deep down, a social relation between people.</p>\n<p>Fetishism does not mean that people worship objects in the ordinary religious sense. It means that social relations appear as natural properties of things. The price, the brand, fashion or the ‘laws’ of the market are seen as if they were autonomous forces; meanwhile, the division of labour, exploitation and the conditions of production remain hidden. That is why fetishism and ideology go together. Capitalism can present itself as a free exchange: independent buyers and sellers meet on the market. But behind that formal exchange, some control the means of production and others have to sell their labour power. Marx’s analysis wants to cut through that appearance.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">‘Everything solid vanishes into air.’ — Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels, <em>The Communist Manifesto</em> (1848), ch. 1</blockquote>\n\n<h3>Ideology: when the real relation is hidden</h3>\n<p data-bloque=\"hf-sospecha-120\">In Marx, ideology is not just a false idea. It is the form that conceals or naturalises real social relations; Engels later called it ‘<strong>false consciousness</strong>’: a consciousness that hides exploitation. The ideas of the ruling class can become the dominant ideas of an age, not necessarily through a conscious lie, but because the very structure of society makes some points of view more credible than others.</p>\n<p>For example, the discourse of <strong>meritocracy</strong> stresses effort and talent, and that has some truth in it. But if it hides the inequalities of the economic structure, starting resources, care work, educational opportunities or job insecurity, it becomes ideological. Not because it is simply false, but because it partly conceals a real relation. Marx’s suspicion thus poses a twofold question: what does this discourse say and what does it hide? What interests does it make seem sensible? What social relations does it leave invisible? Without those questions, consciousness can easily become a reassuring explanation of its own dependence.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Investigate: choose a current discourse —meritocracy, consumption, entrepreneurship or flexible work— and explain what interests or social structures it may hide. Use the concepts of class, ideology, fetishism, labour power and base.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>How ideology works: form</th><th>What does it do?</th><th>Critical example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Naturalisation</td><td>Presents the historical as if it were natural</td><td>‘There have always been rich and poor’</td></tr><tr><td>Individualisation</td><td>Turns a social structure into a personal problem</td><td>Reducing poverty to a lack of effort</td></tr><tr><td>Universalisation</td><td>Presents the interest of one class as the interest of all</td><td>Presenting the market as pure freedom</td></tr><tr><td>Invisibilisation</td><td>Hides labour relations</td><td>Seeing the price of the product, but not its conditions of production</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Ideology is not always a violent command: many times it takes the form of normality.</p>\n\n<h3>Praxis: interpreting and transforming</h3>\n<p>Marx’s philosophy reorganises the relationship between theory and practice. In the eleventh thesis on Feuerbach he says that philosophers have interpreted the world, but that the point is to transform it. That sentence is not a slogan against theory. On the contrary: it indicates that, for theory to be truly critical, it has to be joined to the transformation of society.</p>\n<p><strong>Praxis</strong> is the activity that unites theory and historical action. Understanding society is not just looking at it from outside; that understanding has to open up for subjects the possibility of seeing their situation and changing it. That is why, for Marx, philosophy is not an exercise shut up in consciousness, but a critique that enters into the contradictions of society. But praxis also carries a risk: reducing theory to propaganda or turning political action into dogma. A rigorous reading of Marx has to avoid both extremes. It is not enough to interpret the world, but neither is it enough to change it without critically examining the structure of that change, its consequences and the new powers it creates.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">‘Philosophers have only interpreted the world in various ways, but the point is to transform it.’ — Karl Marx, <em>Theses on Feuerbach</em> (1845), thesis XI</blockquote>\n\n<h3>Revolution, communism and the risk of oversimplifying</h3>\n<p>For Marx, <strong>revolution</strong> is not a mere outburst of rage. It is the process by which the working class understands its historical situation, develops class consciousness and overcomes the private ownership of the means of production. The aim is not to share out poverty, but to overcome the antagonism between classes and the alienation of work. In the Marxist tradition, <strong>communism</strong> is the image of a classless society: the means of production would not be subject to the private capital of a few, and work would not be merely a necessity for survival.</p>\n<p>But at this point we cannot oversimplify. Can revolutionary political power guarantee emancipation, or does it create a new form of domination? How can we avoid bureaucratic elites, political control or ideological dogmatism? These questions are not easy attacks on Marx: they are the condition for taking him seriously. The historical experiences of the 20th century have shown that Marxism cannot become a closed dogma. Its strength lies in offering a critical vocabulary for understanding the crises of capitalism, the casualisation of work, the logic of consumption, financial power or the accumulation of large corporations. But we must also critically examine the historical policies carried out in its name.</p>\n<p>Marx thought that capitalism was doomed by its own contradictions: <strong>surplus value</strong> makes exploitation its normal operation, and competition <strong>concentrates capital</strong> in ever fewer hands. He expected a <strong>growing impoverishment</strong> of the proletariat and revolution in the most industrialised countries. The prediction did not turn out that way: revolution triumphed in agrarian Russia, and in the West the <strong>welfare state</strong> improved workers’ conditions. The thesis that only labour creates value is also disputed. <strong>Popper</strong> would add that, by reinterpreting the theory so that it fitted any fact, its followers made it irrefutable, and for that reason it ceased to be science.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Critical reading of revolution: idea</th><th>Meaning in Marx</th><th>Risk of oversimplification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Revolution</td><td>Transforming the relations of production</td><td>Reducing it to pure political rage</td></tr><tr><td>Communism</td><td>Overcoming the antagonism of classes</td><td>Caricaturing it as shared poverty</td></tr><tr><td>Proletariat</td><td>A class obliged to sell its labour power</td><td>Idealising it as a purely moral subject</td></tr><tr><td>State</td><td>Historical apparatus of class relations</td><td>Thinking that the new power is automatically liberating</td></tr><tr><td>Praxis</td><td>Uniting critique and action</td><td>Turning theory into propaganda</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<h3>Marx today: relevance without propaganda</h3>\n<p>The relevance of Marx does not consist in directly applying all his sentences. Today’s capitalism is not a simple copy of the 19th-century factory: digital platforms, global finance, the data economy, fast consumption and new forms of precarious work have appeared. Even so, his questions remain alive: who controls the new means of production? Who creates the value? Who appropriates the surplus? What ideology makes that structure seem normal?</p>\n<p>On digital platforms, for example, workers may appear as self-employed, but algorithms, customer ratings and the company’s conditions can set the pace of work and the income. In consumption, products appear as symbols of personal freedom, but behind them there are global production chains, unequal working conditions and environmental costs. The question of fetishism appears again: what does the attractive object hide?</p>\n<p>That is why Marx is not a closed recipe, but a toolbox of critical questions. Using his philosophy is not making propaganda. It is cutting through the appearances of modern society: examining what material dependence lies behind formal freedom, what labour relation lies behind the objects of the market and what social interest lies behind the explanations of consciousness.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Not oversimplifying Marx: simplification</th><th>More precise reading</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Marx is pure economics</td><td>He unites economics, philosophy, history and politics</td></tr><tr><td>Ideas do not matter</td><td>Ideas are situated in material conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Alienation is only a psychological feeling</td><td>It arises in the structure of work, property and the market</td></tr><tr><td>Revolution is pure rage</td><td>It is the historical transformation of the relations of production</td></tr><tr><td>Communism is an automatic guarantee</td><td>That project also has political and institutional risks</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<table class=\"glos\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Material critique</td><td>Examining ideas, laws and institutions in relation to their economic and historical conditions.</td></tr><tr><td>Materialist dialectic</td><td>Situating historical contradictions in material conditions: work, property, production and class relations.</td></tr><tr><td>Alienation</td><td>Loss of control over work and its product: the worker becomes estranged from his product, his activity, other people and his own creative capacities.</td></tr><tr><td>Ideology</td><td>A form of consciousness that presents historical and social relations as natural, just or unchangeable; a ‘false consciousness’ that hides exploitation.</td></tr><tr><td>Historical materialism</td><td>A theory that studies how modes of production, class relations and material conditions condition history.</td></tr><tr><td>Base / superstructure</td><td>The relationship between the economic base and the legal, political and cultural forms that rise on it.</td></tr><tr><td>Class struggle</td><td>The historical conflict between classes with opposed interests within a mode of production.</td></tr><tr><td>Surplus value</td><td>Value that the worker produces and does not receive in his wage; one of the bases of the accumulation of capital.</td></tr><tr><td>Fetishism</td><td>Occurs when the relations of labour and power between people appear as natural relations between things.</td></tr><tr><td>Praxis</td><td>Historical activity that unites theory and practical transformation.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n\n<h2>Nietzsche: moral suspicion</h2>\n<h3 data-bloque=\"hf-sospecha-141\">The genealogy of the values of the West</h3>\n<p><strong>Friedrich Nietzsche</strong> (1844-1900) is suspicious of the whole Western tradition and produces the <strong>genealogy</strong> of its values: where do our morality, our truth, our gods come from? Behind ‘piety’, ‘humility’ and ‘equality’ there is no goodness, but <strong>resentment</strong> against life and against the strong. The phrase ‘God is dead’ is not a celebration, but a diagnosis: the supreme values have lost their support, and <strong>nihilism</strong> is at the door. The question is no longer what truth is, but what is of value for living.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">‘God is dead.’ — Friedrich Nietzsche, <em>The Gay Science</em> (1882), § 125</blockquote>\n\n<h3>Schopenhauer: the will, suffering and the shadow of reason</h3>\n<p><strong>Arthur Schopenhauer</strong> sets a sombre counterpoint to Hegel’s historical optimism (<em>The World as Will and Representation</em>, 1819). The world is not the transparent and reconciled development of reason: the world is the expression of the <strong>will</strong>. Kant established the distinction between the phenomenon and the thing in itself. According to Schopenhauer, the thing in itself is not a clear reason, but a <strong>blind will</strong>: a wanting, an impulse and a lack that cannot be satisfied.</p>\n<p>That is why human life appears in a painful tension. We suffer until we get what we want; when we get it, the satisfaction lasts a short time and another desire is born. Suffering and boredom are the two poles of existence. Reason is not a sovereign queen but, many times, a sophisticated servant of desire.</p>\n<p>Schopenhauer’s ethics stresses <strong>compassion</strong>: seeing that the suffering of other beings is bound up with my own. But his way out often takes the direction of <strong>asceticism</strong>, to weaken desire and calm the force of the will. Nietzsche will learn much from Schopenhauer, but in the end will reject his ascetic negation of life. That is why Schopenhauer is a peculiar bridge: he breaks the confidence in modern reason, but does not yet offer Nietzsche’s tragic affirmation.</p>\n\n<h3>Suspicion of Western morality</h3>\n<p>Nietzsche’s philosophy is not a collection of provocations. His central question is sharp: what happens if our great truths, moralities and values do not have a pure and rational origin? Nietzsche places under suspicion the story that Western culture has told about itself. He does not ask only ‘what is good?’, but ‘who has called this good, in what situation and in favour of what kind of life?’.</p>\n<p>That is why we must understand Nietzsche’s first context: the influence of <strong>Schopenhauer</strong> and the critique he made of him, distrust of the progress of history and distance from the revolutionary and Enlightenment ideals. Nietzsche does not believe in a linear historical optimism; he does not believe that reason or revolution will improve the human being by themselves. His first <strong>tragic philosophy</strong> goes against the simple alternative between pessimism and optimism: suffering must be neither denied nor covered over with a saving ideal, but thought within life.</p>\n<p>That is why Nietzsche is not a simple defender of a chaos without morality. He examines concrete moralities in order to bring their origin and consequences to light. Does a morality strengthen life, or does it sanctify weakness, revenge and guilt? Does a truth open up thought, or does it want to lock the diversity of life into a single form? For Nietzsche, doing philosophy is doing it with the <strong>hammer</strong>: he is the ‘philosopher with a hammer’, who strikes the idols, the sacred truths, to hear whether they sound hollow or still have a ring. Among those idols are absolute truth, the transparent subject, the appearance of a universal morality and the transcendent values placed above this world.</p>\n<p>That is why he is often associated with labels such as <strong>irrationalism</strong>, <strong>naturalism</strong> and <strong>vitalism</strong>: not because he rejects reason in a simple way, but because he opposes abstract reason when it hides the body, instinct, affects and vital force. His starting point is vitalism: life (instinct, body, passion) is the only valuable reality.</p>\n\n<h3>The Apollonian and the Dionysian: the tragic sense of life</h3>\n<p>In Nietzsche’s first thought (<em>The Birth of Tragedy</em>, 1872), Greek tragedy is not just an old art form: it is a way of understanding life. <strong>The Apollonian</strong> expresses clarity, form, measure, image and individuality: order and reason. <strong>The Dionysian</strong>, by contrast, expresses excess, the body, impulse, sacred intoxication, a more living unity and creative chaos: instinct.</p>\n<p>In great tragedy, the two forces do not cancel each other out. Apollo gives form to the confused force of the Dionysian; Dionysus, for his part, fills the Apollonian form with the tragic depth of life. That is why, for Nietzsche, the truth of life is not exhausted in transparent concepts: birth and death, flourishing and ruin, joy and suffering go together. Purely rationalist philosophy tends to cover up that dark and fertile side of life.</p>\n<p>According to Nietzsche’s reading, from Socrates onwards Western culture trusted too much in the concept, in reason and in theoretical explanation. That does not mean that thinking is bad; it means that thought is impoverished when it wants to replace life. The West entered into decline when Socratic reason, Plato and later Christianity stifled the Dionysian in order to impose a non-existent ‘true world’. The great lesson of tragedy is this: suffering is not always a defect to be eliminated, but part of the totality of life.</p>\n\n<h3>The death of God and nihilism</h3>\n<p>‘God is dead’ is not an ordinary atheist phrase. For Nietzsche, the phrase expresses that Western culture has lost the foundation of its supreme values. God is not here just a religious figure: he is also the name of absolute truth, of an objective moral order and of a transcendent meaning. When that foundation falls, the old values still remain in language, but have lost their deep ground. When the supreme values fall, the human being is left orphaned of meaning.</p>\n<p>This is where <strong>nihilism</strong> comes from. When human beings stop believing in absolute values, the risk is that everything appears empty, worthless or without direction. Nietzsche does not celebrate nihilism: he diagnoses it. The problem is not only that the old values have fallen, but that human beings may lose the strength to create new values. That is why a <strong>passive nihilism</strong> and an <strong>active nihilism</strong> can be distinguished. The passive kind leads to resignation, weariness and the petty comfort of the ‘last man’. The active kind, by contrast, takes the fall of the old values as an opportunity: the opportunity to overcome the values that denied life and to create new ones.</p>\n<p>In his notes of 1887, Nietzsche also speaks of an <strong>incomplete nihilism</strong>: that of someone who stops believing in God but keeps, under other names, the values that depended on him (fragment 10[42], KSA 12). These are the ‘shadows’ of God, which go on being cast after his death (<em>The Gay Science</em>, § 108): faith in progress, in science as truth without presuppositions or in the morality of compassion. For Nietzsche, these substitutes only postpone the problem: nihilism must be taken all the way to the end in order to overcome it. (The 1887 notes are not a finished work: many were published after his death in the compilation <em>The Will to Power</em>.)</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">‘One must have chaos within oneself to give birth to a dancing star.’ — Friedrich Nietzsche, <em>Thus Spoke Zarathustra</em> (1883), Prologue, 5</blockquote>\n\n<h3>From nihilism to the revaluation of all values</h3>\n<p>Nihilism is not a sudden bad mood. It is the historical consequence of the death of God: when sacred values, transcendent truth and objective morality weaken, the human being faces a hard question: on what should life be based? At that moment two paths open up. One wants to hold on desperately to the remains of the old values; the other takes the ruin as an opportunity.</p>\n<p>The <strong>transmutation</strong> (or revaluation) <strong>of values</strong> is the name of that second path. It does not consist in replacing some values with others under different names, but in changing the very criterion by which values are created. If the morality of slaves extols humility, obedience, guilt and the sanctification of suffering, for Nietzsche the new values must affirm the strength of life, creativity, courage, the body and self-overcoming.</p>\n<p>That is why active nihilism is a dangerous but necessary transition. First we must see that the old values have fallen; then that fall must not be used to stay in the void, but to create new values. Transmutation is not saying ‘everything is permitted’. It is much more demanding: it asks of each value what kind of life it feeds, whether it generates strength or sanctifies weakness.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Investigate: explain how the death of God, passive nihilism, active nihilism and the revaluation of values are related. Use the concepts of absolute values, emptiness, creativity, affirmation of life and overman.</p>\n\n<h3>Genealogy, resentment and the inversion of morality</h3>\n<p>Genealogy is not a neutral history of concepts. Nietzsche investigates the origin of values, but with an uncomfortable question: what forces created them? In <em>On the Genealogy of Morals</em> (1887) he distinguishes <strong>master morality</strong> from <strong>slave morality</strong>. In master morality, ‘good’ is associated with strength, greatness, creativity and the capacity to affirm oneself. ‘Bad’ is what is weak or mediocre, but not necessarily the sinful.</p>\n<p>In slave morality, by contrast, an <strong>inversion of values</strong> takes place. Weakness cannot defeat strength directly and therefore uses the language of morality as a symbolic revenge. The strong become ‘evil’; the humble, the obedient and the suffering become ‘good’. Here <strong><em>ressentiment</em></strong> (resentment) appears: when impotence and hidden revenge are disguised as virtue. That morality is a symptom of a weak and resentful life; against it, master morality is the affirmation of life as it is.</p>\n<p>Nietzsche does not mean that violence in itself is good. His critique is deeper: many moralities make the strength of life suspect, make desire and the body guilty, and present obedience as salvation. Genealogy shows that values have a history, and that in that history there are power, affects and interests. That is why <strong>Platonism</strong> and <strong>Christianity</strong> are two of his main targets. According to Nietzsche, both run the risk of turning this world into something second-rate: by placing the true world, salvation or absolute values elsewhere, they <strong>devalue</strong> or <strong>despise</strong> our world. That does not mean that this is the only possible reading of those traditions, but it does mean that, for Nietzsche, they are the great movement against the affirmation of life.</p>\n<p>In his critique of Christianity, Nietzsche examines the language of sin, guilt and reward after death. In his view, that language makes the impulses of life bad and the desires of the body suspect. When the suffering of this world is justified by salvation in another world, there is a risk of weakening the forces that the human being has on earth. Here genealogy appears again: even when a value seems ‘sacred’, we must ask what origin it has and what vital force it serves.</p>\n<p>In the second part of the <em>Genealogy</em>, Nietzsche explains the origin of <strong>bad conscience</strong>: when society forbids discharging aggressive instincts outwards, they turn inwards; conscience is <strong>internalised cruelty</strong>. In the third part, the <strong>ascetic priest</strong> turns that unease into <strong>guilt</strong> (‘you suffer because you are guilty’) and, through the <strong>ascetic ideal</strong>, gives meaning to suffering. Thus a morality that was born to make life bearable ends up poisoning it. Nietzsche sees in the priest the great organiser of the herd’s resentment.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: when the old values fall, what distinguishes passive nihilism from the capacity to create new values?</p>\n\n<h3>Will to power, perspectivism and truth</h3>\n<p>The <strong>will to power</strong> is not merely the desire to crush others. For Nietzsche, it is the tendency of life to give form, interpret, overcome and organise forces: the impulse to overcome and affirm oneself. Life is not mere preservation; it is also an impulse to grow, to create and to overcome itself. That is why the will to power is an ontological and interpretive concept: living beings do not receive the world neutrally, but interpret it according to their forces.</p>\n<p>With this idea Nietzsche corrects two authors he had admired. Against Darwinism, he maintains that life cannot be reduced to the struggle to survive or to adaptation: the struggle for existence is the exception, and the rule is the struggle to grow and for more power (<em>The Gay Science</em>, § 349; <em>Twilight of the Idols</em>, ‘Skirmishes of an Untimely Man’, § 14). Against Schopenhauer, the will is not a single blind force that ought to be denied, but a plurality of drives that command and obey (<em>Beyond Good and Evil</em>, § 19); and what it wants is not to go on living, but to overcome itself: ‘only where there is life is there also will; but not will to life, rather will to power’ (<em>Thus Spoke Zarathustra</em>, II, ‘On Self-Overcoming’). Where Schopenhauer saw a reason to deny the will, Nietzsche sees a reason to affirm it.</p>\n<p>This is where <strong>perspectivism</strong> comes from. Knowledge is not the result of a ‘view from nowhere’. There are perspectives: bodies, interests, languages and relations of force. But perspectivism does not mean that all opinions are worth the same. Some perspectives may be richer, stronger and more faithful to life; others, impoverishing and life-denying. Nietzsche’s critique of truth does not put an end to thinking. On the contrary, it demands greater responsibility from it: what kind of life does this truth need? What does it hide in the name of objectivity? What does it serve to create, or what does it serve to control?</p>\n<p>This is where the critique of the traditional idea of truth comes in: truth is not always a transparent and neutral copy of things, but a historical web of interpretations, metaphors and perspectives. Human beings name, classify and fix the world, and then forget those fixings as if they were ‘reality itself’. Nietzsche’s suspicion breaks that forgetting: truth is often a hardened <strong>metaphor</strong>, an <strong>interpretation</strong> that has begun to hide its origin.</p>\n<p>Suspicion also reaches <strong>logic</strong> and <strong>science</strong>. For Nietzsche, logic arose as a tool for survival: beings that treated similar cases as equal and drew conclusions quickly had a better chance of living than those that hesitated (<em>The Gay Science</em>, § 111). Concepts such as thing, substance, cause or law are useful fictions, not copies of the world. Nor is physics the last word: it is ‘an interpretation and arrangement of the world’, not an explanation (<em>Beyond Good and Evil</em>, § 14). And science rests on a faith that it cannot justify by its own means: the faith that truth is worth more than anything, which is still the faith of Plato and of Christianity (<em>The Gay Science</em>, § 344). That is why Nietzsche does not reject science, but the idea that it is knowledge without a perspective.</p>\n\n<h3>The overman and eternal recurrence</h3>\n<p>The <strong>overman</strong> is not the caricature of a race, a political leader or a violent hero. Nietzsche presents the human being as something to be overcome: a bridge, not a goal. The overman does not want to live among the remains of the old values; he expresses the capacity to create his own values, without denying life and without returning to the old morality of guilt.</p>\n<p>The <strong>eternal recurrence</strong> can be understood as an existential test. If you had to live your life, with all its moments, over and over again, would you accept it? That question does not seek comfort, but measures the strength of affirmation. Truly affirming life is not eliminating suffering; it is the capacity to accept suffering, conflict and the unending as part of life too.</p>\n<p>Thus Nietzsche is not a mere destroyer. His suspicion dismantles the old values, but the aim is not to stay in the void. The aim is to create values: to leave passive nihilism and move on to the active affirmation of life. That is why he criticises <strong>followers</strong> and <strong>mediocrity</strong>: even if the old values have fallen, if the human being clings to small comforts, to the opinion of the herd or to the security of the last man, he creates nothing true. The figure of the overman is not a fantasy of social superiority, but the existential challenge of leaving mediocrity and imitation behind.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Investigate: explain why Nietzsche criticises Christianity from the genealogy of morals. What kind of life does it strengthen or weaken? Use the concepts of <em>ressentiment</em>, sin, guilt, slave morality, master morality and affirmation of life.</p>\n\n<h3>The camel, the lion and the child: the three metamorphoses of the spirit</h3>\n<p>In the language of Zarathustra, the human being’s path of liberation is explained with three figures: the <strong>camel</strong>, the <strong>lion</strong> and the <strong>child</strong>. The camel bears the load: the commands of tradition, the morality of ‘thou shalt’, obedience and the weight of duty. It is capable of walking through the desert, but still carries the burden of the old values.</p>\n<p>The lion learns to say ‘no’. Its task is liberation: to confront the old command, reject external authority and open up a space for itself. But the lion is not the final goal, because negation is not enough. It can break the old values, but it does not yet have the full innocence and creativity to create new values.</p>\n<p>That is why the child comes. The child expresses play, a new beginning, creativity and the capacity to say ‘yes’. It is not a passive innocence: it is the force of creation. This is how Nietzsche unites the overman, the revaluation of values and the eternal recurrence: after denying the old values, we must create again and affirm life.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>The three metamorphoses of the spirit: figure</th><th>What does it express?</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The camel</td><td>Burden, obedience, duty</td><td>Bears the weight of the old values</td></tr><tr><td>The lion</td><td>Negation, liberation, rejection of authority</td><td>Does not yet create new values</td></tr><tr><td>The child</td><td>Play, creativity, a new beginning</td><td>Has to say yes to risk</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Zarathustra’s figures sum up the process of liberation and the creation of values.</p>\n\n<h3>A diagnosis of Western culture</h3>\n<p>In this survey, Nietzsche appears as a radical critic of Western culture. In his view, Western metaphysics and morality have denied the force of life: they have despised this world and judged it in the name of another stable, perfect or moral world. That is why the ‘death of God’ is not a simple atheist phrase: it is the crisis of a whole system of values. Notions such as truth, the good, the soul, the other world or universal morality are not neutral: they are expressions of an attitude to life.</p>\n<p>Nihilism is the name of that crisis: the old values have lost their strength, but the new ones have not yet been created. Nietzsche’s question is therefore not what we should believe, but how to create values in favour of life, beyond the morality of guilt and negation. That is why he uses genealogy: he does not ask only whether a value is true, but where it comes from, what forces created it and what kind of life it promotes. Morality is not a law handed down from heaven, but a history among forces. Slave morality turns weakness, guilt and resentment into values; master morality, by contrast, turns strength, creation and affirmation into values.</p>\n<p>Nihilism is the ruin of the old values, but it is not the goal. The risk is to stay in the void; the opportunity, to create new values. Here the <strong>will to power</strong>, the <strong>overman</strong> and the <strong>eternal recurrence</strong> come in: instead of denying life, they are tests for affirming it in its totality.</p>\n\n<h3>From Nietzsche to postmodernity</h3>\n<p>Nietzsche’s ‘death of God’ does not express a simple atheism. It means that Western culture has lost the foundation of its absolute values: truth, the good, reason or progress are no longer pillars guaranteed by themselves. That is why <strong>nihilism</strong> appears: the old values lose their force, but the capacity to create new values is not assured.</p>\n<p>Postmodernity takes up that legacy of Nietzsche: the crisis of the great metanarratives, suspicion of a single truth, genealogy and the conflict between interpretations. But Nietzsche is not the excuse for a comfortable relativism. His challenge is more demanding: we must take on the responsibility of creating values, starting from the affirmation of life and from creativity. That is why the path that leads from Nietzsche to postmodernity (Topic 23) is not a mere chronology, but the need to examine the foundations of modernity again.</p>\n<p>When reading Nietzsche it is advisable to keep three cautions in mind. Genealogy is a plausible <strong>hypothesis</strong>, not a demonstration; and the fact that a value has a petty origin does not prove that it is false (that would be a <strong>genetic fallacy</strong>). His perspectivism raises a problem of <strong>self-reference</strong>: if there are no moral facts or truths, Nietzsche cannot claim for his own evaluation the status that he denies to others. And <em>The Will to Power</em> is not a book of his, but a posthumous anthology put together in the archive of his sister <strong>Elisabeth</strong>, a militant antisemite; Nietzsche despised antisemitism.</p>\n<h3>Nietzsche: genealogy, nihilism and new values</h3><p>Nietzsche demands a deeper diagnosis of culture. The ‘death of God’ is not only the end of a religious belief; it is the weakening of the foundation of the whole Western system of values. Concepts such as truth, the good, the soul, the other world or universal morality are not neutral: they express a stance taken towards life.</p><p>That is why Nietzsche uses <strong>genealogy</strong>. He does not only ask whether a value is true; he asks where it comes from, what forces created it and what kind of life it promotes. Morality, then, is not a law handed down from heaven, but a history of forces in conflict. Slave morality turns weakness, guilt and resentment into values; master morality, by contrast, turns strength, creation and affirmation into values.</p>\n<table class=\"glos\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Death of God</td><td>A formula expressing the decline of absolute values and transcendent foundations. It opens up the problem of nihilism.</td></tr><tr><td>Blind will</td><td>For Schopenhauer, at the bottom of reality there is not a transparent reason, but an impulse and an insatiable wanting without a rational end.</td></tr><tr><td>Representation</td><td>The way the world appears to the subject. We do not know the thing in itself directly, but the world as it appears in our forms of knowledge.</td></tr><tr><td>Overman</td><td>One who creates his own values: a creative vital force capable of creating new values. Not a political caricature, but the image of the human being’s self-overcoming.</td></tr><tr><td>Will to power</td><td>An impulse to overcome and affirm oneself. It does not mean merely brute domination: it expresses the creative force of life and the capacity to impose new forms.</td></tr><tr><td>Genealogy of morals</td><td>A critical method that studies moral values according to their historical, affective and power-related origin.</td></tr><tr><td>Perspectivism</td><td>Stresses that all knowledge is situated. It does not mean that all opinions are worth the same, but that absolute discourses disguise their own origin.</td></tr><tr><td>Nihilism</td><td>Emptiness of meaning when the supreme values fall. It can be passive (resignation) or active (an opportunity to create new values).</td></tr><tr><td>The Apollonian</td><td>A force that expresses clarity, form, measure, image and individuality. In tragedy it appears alongside the Dionysian.</td></tr><tr><td>The Dionysian</td><td>Expresses excess, the body, impulse, creative chaos and the tragic unity of life.</td></tr><tr><td>Revaluation of values</td><td>The process of overcoming the old values that deny life and creating new values that affirm the force of life, creativity and self-overcoming.</td></tr><tr><td><em>Ressentiment</em></td><td>Hidden revenge born of impotence: it turns the strong into the evil and presents weakness as virtue.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n\n<h2>Freud: psychic suspicion</h2>\n<h3>The third wound</h3>\n<p data-bloque=\"hf-sospecha-202\"><strong>Sigmund Freud</strong> (1856-1939) looks into the interior of the individual and inflicts on human beings a third narcissistic wound: we are not even masters in our own house. We are guided, more than we think, by the <strong>unconscious</strong>, desire and <strong>repression</strong>. Consciousness is only a small part of psychic life (it is usually explained with the image of the iceberg, of which consciousness would be only the tip); most of the psyche is unconscious, governed by repressed desires. Against the tradition that said consciousness was transparent, <strong>psychoanalysis</strong> is the third great school of suspicion.</p>\n<p>Freud calls his theory the third great <strong>humiliation</strong> of the human being: after Copernicus (we are not the centre of the universe) and Darwin (we are related to the primates), Freud shows that we do not even control our own rationality.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">‘The interpretation of dreams is the royal road to the knowledge of the unconscious.’ — Sigmund Freud, <em>The Interpretation of Dreams</em> (1900), ch. VII</blockquote>\n\n<h3>The breaking of the transparent subject</h3>\n<p>Freud’s philosophical importance lies not only in having proposed a therapeutic method. His blow is deeper: the subject is not the transparent master of himself. Modernity often thought that consciousness itself was the place of certainty; Freud, by contrast, shows that consciousness does not exhaust the psychic life of the subject. What the human being says, what he believes he wants and what he does do not always coincide. Symptoms, dreams, slips of the tongue, repetitions or incomprehensible behaviour are not mere noise: they can be traces of a meaning.</p>\n<p>That is why Freud is one of the masters of suspicion: he shows that beneath the surface account of consciousness there is another logic. The unconscious is not a simple ‘dark storeroom’. It has its rules: desire, displacement, condensation, censorship and indirect returns. That is why the subject is not a single block: it is a field of psychic conflicts.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">‘The ego is not master in its own house.’ — Sigmund Freud, <em>A Difficulty in the Path of Psycho-Analysis</em> (1917)</blockquote>\n\n<h3>Dreams, slips and the language of the symptom</h3>\n<p>For Freud, the <strong>dream</strong> is one of the main roads to the unconscious, but not directly. The dream often expresses a desire, but that desire appears disguised. The manifest content of the dream is not enough; beneath it there is psychic work: <strong>condensation</strong>, <strong>displacement</strong> and <strong>censorship</strong>. That is why the dream must be interpreted.</p>\n<p><strong>Slips</strong> and <strong>symptoms</strong> follow a similar logic. A word someone says unintentionally, a forgetting or a bodily symptom does not always have a simple conscious explanation. According to Freud, repression does not make the content disappear: what has been set aside returns by another route. The symptom can be a compromise formation: it expresses a desire, but deformed in such a way that censorship can accept it.</p>\n<p>This does not mean that anything can mean anything. Psychoanalysis demands rigorous interpretation: we must take into account the subject’s history, conflicts, language and repetitions. Freud shows that meaning is not always exhausted in what is expressed on the surface.</p>\n\n<h3>The id, the ego and the superego</h3>\n<p>Freud’s <strong>second topography</strong> (<em>The Ego and the Id</em>, 1923) presents the subject as the tension between three agencies. The <strong>id</strong> is the field of drives, of instincts: desire, drive energy and the pressure that seeks satisfaction. It follows neither a moral logic nor a direct calculation of reality; it is bound to the <strong>pleasure principle</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>ego</strong> is not an all-powerful ruler: it is the reason that mediates with reality, a mediator in a difficult position. It has to manage the drives of the id, the limits of external reality and the prohibitions of the superego. That is why the ego often resorts to <strong>defence mechanisms</strong>: repression, projection, displacement or rationalisation. The ego does not deceive itself just because it is a liar; it deceives itself because, by its structure, it stands between opposing pressures.</p>\n<p>The <strong>superego</strong> is the agency of internalised norms, prohibitions and ideals: internalised moral norms. It is not a mere external policeman; it is a moral and social demand located inside the subject. That is why it can generate guilt and self-punishment. For Freud, the modern subject is not the kingdom of a transparent freedom, but the organisation of a permanent inner conflict.</p>\n<p>The forces that drive that conflict are the <strong>drives</strong>, which Freud finally reduces to two: <strong>Eros</strong> (the life drive) and <strong>Thanatos</strong> (the death drive).</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: if, according to Freud, the ego is not master of its own house, how must the idea of freedom and responsibility change?</p>\n\n<h3>Culture, repression and discontent</h3>\n<p>Culture gives us security, language, cooperation and coexistence. But culture is not a free gain. In order to live together we have to limit, postpone or channel some drives. From this is born the <strong>discontent in civilisation</strong> (<em>Civilization and Its Discontents</em>, 1930): civilisation protects us, but demands that we sacrifice part of our desire. To live in society we repress desires, and that repression generates <strong>neurosis</strong>: the human being can never be fully happy in civilisation.</p>\n<p>The pleasure principle seeks quick satisfaction; the <strong>reality principle</strong>, by contrast, imposes postponement, calculation and limits. Adult life unfolds in the tension between the two. If culture becomes too rigid, guilt, neurosis and self-punishment may increase; if there are no limits, coexistence itself is endangered.</p>\n<p><strong>Sublimation</strong> is one of the main outlets for that tension. When a drive cannot be satisfied directly, it can be channelled towards another activity: art, knowledge, creation or cultural work. For Freud, culture itself is, to a certain extent, a transformation of drive energy. That is why psychoanalysis does not reduce the human being to a mere animal: it thinks culture and desire in mutual dependence.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Investigate: explain what the idea that ‘the ego is not master in its own house’ means. How does that change our understanding of freedom and responsibility? Use the concepts of the unconscious, repression, id, ego, superego, symptom and self-knowledge.</p>\n<table class=\"glos\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Unconscious</td><td>Repressed desires and conflicts that govern us; most of psychic life.</td></tr><tr><td>Id / Ego / Superego</td><td>Instincts / reason / moral norms (Freud): the three agencies of the second topography.</td></tr><tr><td>Repression</td><td>A mechanism that removes an unacceptable or conflictual content from consciousness. The content does not disappear: it can return as a symptom or as a dream.</td></tr><tr><td>Discontent in civilisation</td><td>The thesis that society provides security and coexistence, but also generates control of desire, guilt and costly inner limits.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n\n<h2>Ricoeur: masters of suspicion?</h2>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">‘The symbol gives food for thought.’ — Paul Ricoeur, <em>The Symbolism of Evil</em> (1960), conclusion</blockquote>\n<h3>One label for three authors</h3>\n<p data-bloque=\"hf-sospecha-228\"><strong>Paul Ricoeur</strong> (1913-2005) coined the label ‘masters of suspicion’ to read Marx, Nietzsche and Freud together (<em>Freud and Philosophy: An Essay on Interpretation</em>, 1965). All three call into doubt the transparency of consciousness; but, for Ricoeur, suspicion is not the last word: after critique comes <strong>hermeneutics</strong>, to recover meaning. This section closes the topic by weighing the contribution and the limits of the three masters.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: when does a unifying concept help, and when does it become an impoverishing summary that replaces thought?</p>\n\n<h3>Ricoeur’s formula: ‘masters of suspicion’</h3>\n<p>Paul Ricoeur grouped Marx, Nietzsche and Freud as <strong>masters of suspicion</strong> because all three call into question the modern image of a transparent consciousness. The subject of the Enlightenment wanted to understand itself as rational, autonomous and transparent; these three authors, by contrast, sought other forces beneath surface consciousness.</p>\n<p>Ricoeur’s formula does not say that the authors are the same. His gesture names a resemblance of method: the manifest meaning is not the last word. We must examine what a discourse says about itself, but also what it hides, in what conditions it arises and what forces make it credible.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Suspicion yes, but not the same: author</th><th>Object of suspicion</th><th>Method</th><th>Risk of the simple label</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Marx</td><td>Ideology and social consciousness</td><td>Historical materialism and critique of political economy</td><td>Reducing everything to economics</td></tr><tr><td>Nietzsche</td><td>The hidden values of morality and truth</td><td>Genealogy and critique of culture</td><td>Reading him as if he were pure irrationalism</td></tr><tr><td>Freud</td><td>The transparency of the rational ego</td><td>Psychoanalysis and clinical interpretation</td><td>Reducing him to mere psychologism</td></tr><tr><td>Ricoeur</td><td>The problem of interpretation</td><td>Hermeneutics</td><td>Equating three different projects</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Ricoeur’s label must be understood and, at the same time, limited.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>The three masters face to face</th><th>Marx</th><th>Nietzsche</th><th>Freud</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Suspicious of…</td><td>Ideas and religion</td><td>Morality and truth</td><td>Rationality</td></tr><tr><td>Behind it there is…</td><td>The ideology of the ruling class</td><td>The resentment of the weak</td><td>The unconscious</td></tr><tr><td>Aim</td><td>Classless revolution</td><td>Overman and revaluation of values</td><td>Mental health and self-knowledge</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<h3>Ricoeur’s label: useful, but risky</h3>\n<p>The label ‘masters of suspicion’ is pedagogically powerful, but it must be subjected to criticism. Marx uses suspicion in a <strong>historical-material</strong> way; Nietzsche, in a <strong>genealogical</strong> way; Freud, in a <strong>psychic and clinical</strong> way. The three should not be locked into the same formula: the label is useful, but we must remember that it opens the self-criticism of modernity in three directions.</p>\n<table class=\"glos\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Masters of suspicion</td><td>A name that brings together Marx, Nietzsche and Freud. All three break the transparency of consciousness, but at different levels and with different aims.</td></tr><tr><td>Hermeneutics</td><td>To understand is not to receive a clean piece of data: it is to interpret texts, symbols, symptoms and discourses.</td></tr><tr><td>After suspicion</td><td>Once the critique has been made, the question is not only what deception has been broken, but what meaning, institution or practice can be rebuilt.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"figwrap\"><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/svg/infografia_21-maestros-de-la-sospecha.svg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1116\" alt=\"Infographic: Masters of suspicion\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Infographic: Masters of suspicion</figcaption></figure></div><h2>To think about: bring the question into your life</h2><p class=\"aporia\">When you defend an idea very forcefully, are you sure it is for the reasons you give, or might there be something behind it: an interest, your upbringing, a desire you do not acknowledge? <em>Hint — the ‘masters of suspicion’ (Marx, Nietzsche, Freud) taught us to ask what hides behind ideas: social class, the will to power or unconscious desire. Trick case: being suspicious of others does not free you from being suspicious of yourself.</em></p><p class=\"aporia\">Where do you get your ideas of ‘good’ and ‘bad’ from? And what if some came from the interests of whoever held power when they were formed? <em>Hint — Nietzsche, in On the Genealogy of Morality, proposes tracing the historical origin of values instead of taking them as eternal; pose it as an open question, not as a verdict.</em></p>"}});
